  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nUpper level ridging continues across the region which has\nresulted in warmer temperatures across the state. Temperatures\nare currently warming into the 70s across the state with lower \n80s possible across the southwest. Tonight, low temperatures are\nforecast to drop down around the upper 40s to 50s. Some model \nguidance was hinting at low stratus forming across the eastern \nhalf of the state moving up into the James River Valley tonight, \nhowever model guidance has backed off on this solution at this \ntime.\n\nUpper level ridging will break down this week as a trough \ndeepens across western CONUS. This will bring forth southwest \nflow across the region and a pattern shift. Tomorrow, \ntemperatures are forecast to warm in to the 70s and 80s in the \nsoutheast, however the northwest will warm into the mid 60s as a\ncold front move through the region. There could be some \ncompressional warming ahead of the front so mid 80s are not off \nthe table. The cold front will bring some breezy conditions \nacross the state as the front moves through the region. Moving \ninto Sunday, there are chances for rain across the west and \nportions of the central half of the state. There will likely be \na window Sunday morning into the afternoon where rain chances \nwill remain lower before another wave moves through the region. \nThe upper level wave will move through the region Sunday night \ninto Monday morning. The highest QPF remains across the western\nhalf of the state as there are decent chances for half an inch \nof rain. Temperatures are forecast to drop down into the 40s to \n50s across the region Monday and Tuesday with a very low chance \nof snow mixing in the west Monday morning. Overcast skies could\nkeep temperatures from tanking Tuesday morning however \nwidespread freezing temperatures are possible across the \nnorthwest.\n\nAfter this wave moves through the region Ensemble guidances has \nsplit flow regime with the possibility of the southern stream \nmoving into the area. The GEFs solutions hints at the Northern \nstream bringing in stronger winds later next week. We will \ncontinue to monitor this, check back for the latest forecast \nupdates.",
